 # tags: rfd:details-title, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:06Z (RFD66606-2381) 'The emulator's Windows host answers four system calls as its Linux host does:
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-2381) '- `brk` keeps the new break.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-2381) '- `clock_gettime` writes the 64-bit guest's two 64-bit fields.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-2381) '- Error numbers map to the Linux numbers in one header.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-2381) '- `sched_getaffinity` reports one CPU, as the Linux and macOS hosts do.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-2381) '- RFD66606-2294, where this rule lived before the split.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-2381) 'What went wrong
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-2381) 'The emulator's Windows host answered four system calls differently from its Linux host:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-2381) '- `brk` did not keep the new break. Static glibc puts the main thread's thread-local block at the first break, so the heap handed out memory inside it and, later, an address in read-only data. This was the crash.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-2381) '- `clock_gettime` wrote the host's own `timespec`, a 32-bit nanosecond field with padding, where the 64-bit guest reads two 64-bit fields.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-2381) '- Error numbers above `ERANGE` follow the Windows C runtime (`ENOSYS` is 40, where Linux has 38). glibc chooses its `rseq` and `clone3` fallbacks on `ENOSYS`, so the host now maps errors to the Linux numbers in one header.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-2381) '- `sched_getaffinity` was missing. The Linux and macOS hosts report one CPU, and the Windows host now does the same.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-2381) 'How the break was found
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-31T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-2381) 'A dump of the guest registers and the page's permissions at the fault found the break. The fixes are in our libriscv fork, and the addon takes them with Linux's `ENOSYS` for an unknown system call.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2381  1 LINE
 Text:Written

